% What the virtual memory account says, and what it does not say.
%
% Run through `xpost -d null` by run-ps-test.sh: prints SUCCESS iff every
% assertion holds.
%
% Three numbers describe a bank, and each answers a different question. They
% are easy to mistake for one another, and a reader who mistakes them draws
% the wrong conclusion from a perfectly healthy run, so they are pinned here.
%
%   used     the bytes the bank has taken from fresh storage. It is a cursor
%            and never falls: a collection puts space back on a free list the
%            bank keeps, and does not wind the cursor back. So the difference
%            in used across an operation is the space the operation had to be
%            given NEW because nothing already released could serve it -- not
%            what it allocated, and not what it holds.
%
%   maximum  the storage the bank holds. It grows on demand and never
%            shrinks, so it is also the largest the bank has ever been.
%
%   free     the bytes on the bank's free lists: recovered, still inside the
%            storage the bank holds, and available to an allocation of a size
%            they suit. Reported by .vmfreebytes.
%
% The account an embedder wants is maximum less free: what the bank holds,
% less what it is holding for nothing. Neither vmstatus figure gives that on
% its own, which is why the third number exists.
%
% COLLECTION: this reads the numbers around a collection it asks for itself,
% and asserts the direction each one moves rather than any absolute figure, so
% it does not depend on when a collection would otherwise have run.

/failcount 0 def
/assert {
    exch { pop }
    { (FAIL: ) print print (\n) print /failcount failcount 1 add store }
    ifelse
} bind def

/.vmfreebytes 1183615869 internaldict /.vmfreebytes get def
/used { vmstatus pop exch pop } bind def
/arena { vmstatus exch pop exch pop } bind def
/freebytes { .vmfreebytes pop } bind def

% ---- the three move only in the directions they are allowed to -----------

/u0 used def
/a0 arena def
/f0 freebytes def

a0 u0 ge (the storage a bank holds is at least the cursor into it) assert
f0 a0 le (the free lists cannot hold more than the bank does) assert

% Take a known amount, in pieces a single allocation can hold, then drop
% every reference to it and collect.
/hold 60 array def
0 1 59 { hold exch 60000 array put } for

/u1 used def
/a1 arena def
u1 u0 ge (taking storage does not lower the cursor) assert
a1 a0 ge (taking storage does not shrink the bank) assert
u1 u0 sub 2000000 gt (taking many arrays moves the cursor by a real amount) assert

/hold null def
1 vmreclaim

/u2 used def
/a2 arena def
/f2 freebytes def

% The cursor does not come back. This is the deviation the COMPLIANCE note
% records, and it is asserted rather than merely described, so that a change
% to it is a failure here and not a surprise somewhere else.
u2 u1 ge (a collection does not lower the cursor) assert

% Nor does the bank give its storage back. What a collection recovers stays
% inside the bank, which is why a context's largest moment is its permanent
% size.
a2 a1 ge (a collection does not shrink the bank) assert

% And the recovered space is reported. This is the assertion that makes the
% other two readable: without it, "the numbers did not move" would be
% indistinguishable from "nothing was recovered".
f2 f0 gt (a collection puts the space it recovers on the free lists) assert

% Not merely reported but reported at something like its size, so a figure
% that has gone blind or been truncated fails. Sixty arrays of sixty thousand
% elements is at least fourteen million bytes at the narrower object width;
% the assertion asks for a quarter of that, which no rounding or bookkeeping
% difference reaches.
f2 f0 sub 3500000 gt
    (the free lists report the space at something like its size) assert

% ---- and the account that follows from them ------------------------------
%
% What the bank is using is what it holds less what it holds for nothing.
% After giving up that much, the bank is using far less than it holds, and
% that gap is the thing no single number here reports.

a2 f2 sub a2 lt (a bank holding free space is using less than it holds) assert
a2 f2 sub 0 ge (what a bank is using is not negative) assert

failcount 0 eq { (SUCCESS\n) print }
{ (FAILURES: ) print failcount 8 string cvs print (\n) print } ifelse
flush
